[2026-03-14T22:44:44.962Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RYpFj28rnEAQBgiYtZTGiK","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Overview\n     2→\n     3→This repository is a platform for agentic app building. You will build and/or maintain one or more\n     4→web apps according to a set of skills and guidelines which will evolve as you get better at building apps.\n     5→\n     6→Key directories:\n     7→\n     8→* `apps`: Has one subdirectory for each app that has been built or has been specified and still needs to be built.\n     9→* `skills/messages`: Skills for responding to messages from the user (e.g. bug reports, log analysis).\n    10→* `skills/tasks`: Skills for performing tasks. See `skills/AGENTS.md` for details.\n    11→* `reports`: Final generated reports from log analysis (see `skills/review/`).\n    12→* `report-data`: Intermediate analysis data used during report generation.\n    13→* `tasks`: The task queue (`tasks-<containerName>.json`) managed by scripts in `scripts/`.\n    14→* `logs`: Log files from work that has been performed. `worker-current.log` is the log for\n    15→  the work currently being done.\n    16→\n    17→You are running within a container and can run test suites for applications and connect to various\n    18→external services using instructions in the relevant skill files.\n    19→\n    20→**CRITICAL — FIRST THING YOU DO when responding to a user message:**\n    21→\n    22→1. **BEFORE doing ANY work**, list the files in `skills/messages/` and read any skill\n    23→   whose name matches the user's request.\n    24→2. If a matching skill exists, follow it exactly.\n    25→3. Only if no matching skill exists should you proceed on your own — and then write a new\n    26→   skill document in `skills/messages/` afterward so you handle it correctly next time.\n    27→\n    28→Do NOT run git commands, search code, or take any other action until you have checked for a\n    29→matching skill. This is the highest-priority rule when handling user messages.\n    30→\n    31→Do NOT modify this AGENTS.md file unless the user explicitly instructs you to.\n    32→All domain-specific guidance belongs in skill files under `skills/`.\n    33→\n    34→## Worker Execution\n    35→\n    36→The worker processes tasks from `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json` sequentially. For each task,\n    37→it passes all subtasks to Claude as a single prompt and checks for a `<DONE>` signal.\n    38→\n    39→- If `<DONE>` is signaled: the task is dequeued and the worker moves to the next task.\n    40→- If `<DONE>` is NOT signaled: the task is retried (up to 3 times before being skipped).\n    41→\n    42→The worker commits changes after each task automatically. Focus on completing the\n    43→subtasks — do not worry about committing or exiting.\n    44→\n    45→When you have completed ALL subtasks in your task, output `<DONE>` to signal completion.\n    46→\n    47→## Task System\n    48→\n    49→Work is managed through a JSON task queue at `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json`. The file contains an object\n    50→with a `tasks` array. Each task has a `skill`, an array of `subtasks` (description strings),\n    51→a `timestamp`, and an optional `app` name:\n    52→\n    53→```json\n    54→{\n    55→  \"tasks\": [\n    56→    {\n    57→      \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/checkDirectives.md\",\n    58→      \"subtasks\": [\n    59→        \"CheckTestSpecAuth: Check testSpec.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n    60→        \"CheckComponentsAuth: Check writeApp.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n    61→        \"CheckTestsAuth: Check writeTests.md directive violations in Authentication\"\n    62→      ],\n    63→      \"timestamp\": \"2026-02-20T00:00:00.000Z\",\n    64→      \"app\": \"sales-crm\"\n    65→    }\n    66→  ]\n    67→}\n    68→```\n    69→\n    70→The agent NEVER reads or writes task files directly. Instead, use `add-task` via stdin heredoc:\n    71→\n    72→```bash\n    73→npx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n    74→[\n    75→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"SetupApp: Setup the app\", \"DesignDatabase: Design the database\"] },\n    76→  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"WriteTestAuth: Write test for Auth\"] }\n    77→]\n    78→EOF\n    79→```\n    80→\n    81→Tasks are inserted at the front of the queue in the order listed (first element = first to run).\n    82→Each task object must have `skill` and `subtasks`. `app` is optional.\n    83→\n    84→**CRITICAL: `add-task` usage rules**\n    85→\n    86→- When unpacking tasks, put ALL new tasks in a **single `add-task` call**. Do NOT call\n    87→  `add-task` multiple times.\n    88→- Write out every task explicitly in the JSON array. Do NOT use loops, scripts, or code\n    89→  to generate tasks programmatically.\n    90→- All subtasks in a task share the same skill. Group related subtasks together — for example,\n    91→  all checks for a single page go in one task.\n    92→- When a skill needs to \"unpack\" into sub-tasks, use `add-task` to insert them at the\n    93→  front of the queue.\n    94→\n    95→**CRITICAL: Task scope rules**\n    96→\n    97→- You MUST only work on subtasks that were assigned to you in your current prompt. Your assigned\n    98→  subtasks are the ones passed to you by the worker — nothing else.\n    99→- When you add new tasks via `add-task`, you MUST NOT start working on those tasks.\n   100→  They will be picked up by a future worker iteration.\n   101→- If you have completed all of your assigned subtasks and the only remaining work is tasks you\n   102→  added to the queue, you are DONE. Commit your changes and output `<DONE>` immediately.\n   103→  Do NOT continue working on the newly queued tasks.\n   104→- Adding tasks to the queue is NOT the same as doing those tasks. Add them and stop.\n   105→\n   106→## Tech Stack\n   107→\n   108→- **Vite** for build tooling\n   109→- **TypeScript** for all source code (strict mode)\n   110→- **React 18** for UI (single-page application)\n   111→- **Redux** for client side state management\n   112→- **shadcn/ui** for components\n   113→- **Neon** for database backend (Postgres).\n   114→- **Netlify Functions** for any backend/serverless functions. Place functions in `netlify/functions/`. Use `netlify dev` for local development.\n   115→- **File Storage** use UploadThing with the provided token for any file storage.\n   116→\n   117→## Database\n   118→\n   119→All database accesses must happen in backend Netlify functions. Netlify functions should be focused\n   120→and operate on specific parts of the database corresponding to the needs of one or more specific\n   121→frontend components.\n   122→\n   123→## Running Tests\n   124→\n   125→You MUST read `skills/tasks/build/testing.md` and precisely follow its instructions when running\n   126→tests and debugging test failures.\n   127→\n   128→## Quality Gates\n   129→\n   130→Before each commit, run `npm run check` (see `skills/scripts/check.md`). Do not commit\n   131→code that fails typecheck or lint.\n   132→\n   133→## Commits\n   134→\n   135→The worker commits automatically after each iteration. Do not commit manually unless\n   136→explicitly asked to by a skill or prompt.\n   137→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2246777-4cd6-42ce-8777-30d908ca9f10","uuid":"365fba3f-3b61-4547-b131-a5b4c980c0e0","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/repo/AGENTS.md","content":"# Overview\n\nThis repository is a platform for agentic app building. You will build and/or maintain one or more\nweb apps according to a set of skills and guidelines which will evolve as you get better at building apps.\n\nKey directories:\n\n* `apps`: Has one subdirectory for each app that has been built or has been specified and still needs to be built.\n* `skills/messages`: Skills for responding to messages from the user (e.g. bug reports, log analysis).\n* `skills/tasks`: Skills for performing tasks. See `skills/AGENTS.md` for details.\n* `reports`: Final generated reports from log analysis (see `skills/review/`).\n* `report-data`: Intermediate analysis data used during report generation.\n* `tasks`: The task queue (`tasks-<containerName>.json`) managed by scripts in `scripts/`.\n* `logs`: Log files from work that has been performed. `worker-current.log` is the log for\n  the work currently being done.\n\nYou are running within a container and can run test suites for applications and connect to various\nexternal services using instructions in the relevant skill files.\n\n**CRITICAL — FIRST THING YOU DO when responding to a user message:**\n\n1. **BEFORE doing ANY work**, list the files in `skills/messages/` and read any skill\n   whose name matches the user's request.\n2. If a matching skill exists, follow it exactly.\n3. Only if no matching skill exists should you proceed on your own — and then write a new\n   skill document in `skills/messages/` afterward so you handle it correctly next time.\n\nDo NOT run git commands, search code, or take any other action until you have checked for a\nmatching skill. This is the highest-priority rule when handling user messages.\n\nDo NOT modify this AGENTS.md file unless the user explicitly instructs you to.\nAll domain-specific guidance belongs in skill files under `skills/`.\n\n## Worker Execution\n\nThe worker processes tasks from `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json` sequentially. For each task,\nit passes all subtasks to Claude as a single prompt and checks for a `<DONE>` signal.\n\n- If `<DONE>` is signaled: the task is dequeued and the worker moves to the next task.\n- If `<DONE>` is NOT signaled: the task is retried (up to 3 times before being skipped).\n\nThe worker commits changes after each task automatically. Focus on completing the\nsubtasks — do not worry about committing or exiting.\n\nWhen you have completed ALL subtasks in your task, output `<DONE>` to signal completion.\n\n## Task System\n\nWork is managed through a JSON task queue at `tasks/tasks-<containerName>.json`. The file contains an object\nwith a `tasks` array. Each task has a `skill`, an array of `subtasks` (description strings),\na `timestamp`, and an optional `app` name:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"tasks\": [\n    {\n      \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/maintain/checkDirectives.md\",\n      \"subtasks\": [\n        \"CheckTestSpecAuth: Check testSpec.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n        \"CheckComponentsAuth: Check writeApp.md directive violations in Authentication\",\n        \"CheckTestsAuth: Check writeTests.md directive violations in Authentication\"\n      ],\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-02-20T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"app\": \"sales-crm\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe agent NEVER reads or writes task files directly. Instead, use `add-task` via stdin heredoc:\n\n```bash\nnpx tsx /repo/scripts/add-task.ts <<'EOF'\n[\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeApp.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"SetupApp: Setup the app\", \"DesignDatabase: Design the database\"] },\n  { \"skill\": \"skills/tasks/build/writeTests.md\", \"app\": \"SalesCRM\", \"subtasks\": [\"WriteTestAuth: Write test for Auth\"] }\n]\nEOF\n```\n\nTasks are inserted at the front of the queue in the order listed (first element = first to run).\nEach task object must have `skill` and `subtasks`. `app` is optional.\n\n**CRITICAL: `add-task` usage rules**\n\n- When unpacking tasks, put ALL new tasks in a **single `add-task` call**. Do NOT call\n  `add-task` multiple times.\n- Write out every task explicitly in the JSON array. Do NOT use loops, scripts, or code\n  to generate tasks programmatically.\n- All subtasks in a task share the same skill. Group related subtasks together — for example,\n  all checks for a single page go in one task.\n- When a skill needs to \"unpack\" into sub-tasks, use `add-task` to insert them at the\n  front of the queue.\n\n**CRITICAL: Task scope rules**\n\n- You MUST only work on subtasks that were assigned to you in your current prompt. Your assigned\n  subtasks are the ones passed to you by the worker — nothing else.\n- When you add new tasks via `add-task`, you MUST NOT start working on those tasks.\n  They will be picked up by a future worker iteration.\n- If you have completed all of your assigned subtasks and the only remaining work is tasks you\n  added to the queue, you are DONE. Commit your changes and output `<DONE>` immediately.\n  Do NOT continue working on the newly queued tasks.\n- Adding tasks to the queue is NOT the same as doing those tasks. Add them and stop.\n\n## Tech Stack\n\n- **Vite** for build tooling\n- **TypeScript** for all source code (strict mode)\n- **React 18** for UI (single-page application)\n- **Redux** for client side state management\n- **shadcn/ui** for components\n- **Neon** for database backend (Postgres).\n- **Netlify Functions** for any backend/serverless functions. Place functions in `netlify/functions/`. Use `netlify dev` for local development.\n- **File Storage** use UploadThing with the provided token for any file storage.\n\n## Database\n\nAll database accesses must happen in backend Netlify functions. Netlify functions should be focused\nand operate on specific parts of the database corresponding to the needs of one or more specific\nfrontend components.\n\n## Running Tests\n\nYou MUST read `skills/tasks/build/testing.md` and precisely follow its instructions when running\ntests and debugging test failures.\n\n## Quality Gates\n\nBefore each commit, run `npm run check` (see `skills/scripts/check.md`). Do not commit\ncode that fails typecheck or lint.\n\n## Commits\n\nThe worker commits automatically after each iteration. Do not commit manually unless\nexplicitly asked to by a skill or prompt.\n","numLines":137,"startLine":1,"totalLines":137}}}
